<section class="page-head">
  <p class="kicker">{{ site.researchByUrl[page.url].kicker }}</p>
  <h1>JWST&rsquo;s Little Red Dots, Through TTI</h1>
  <p class="lede muted">Suppose the dots are the youngest things we can see because they are also the oldest
    things still arriving: residues of a previous universe, carried through the hidden interval between
    minus one and zero and projected into ours as compact red seams.</p>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame frame--first illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">The stronger speculation in one sentence</p>
    <h2 id="t-proposal">An Integration Front Carrying an Older Residue</h2>
    <p class="lede">A little red dot may be the positive-side appearance of something that did not begin in
      this universe: a prior cosmic residue returning from \(-1\), moving through \((-1,0)\), and crossing
      the zero seam as a seed around which the new universe rapidly integrates.</p>
    <p>On the positive side, the event would still look like the local process already proposed here: a young
      galactic nucleus climbing from the dark-matter-like middle state \(\chi\simeq\tfrac12\) toward luminous
      completion at \(\chi\simeq1\). The new thought is that the climb is <em>triggered or pre-patterned</em>
      by an inherited negative-side residue rather than beginning from featureless initial conditions.</p>
    <p>The black hole would be the closure knot where the old residue acquires a new physical body. The dense
      red source around it would be the seam&rsquo;s visible aftermath: ordinary gas heated and reprocessed by
      an object whose boundary condition is older than its host galaxy. The dot is not the previous galaxy
      surviving intact. It is what that galaxy, universe, or global residue leaves when almost everything
      except its capacity to seed structure has been stripped away. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p class="muted small">Grading on this page: <span class="sci">&#9670; the minimal observational anchor</span>
      &middot; <span class="spec">&#9670; a conditional TTI mechanism</span> &middot;
      <span class="myth">&#9670; philosophical image only</span>. Neither a previous universe nor a physical
      \((-1,0)\) sector is established by astronomy. Both are new extensions of the framework.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">The sky, kept brief</p>
    <h2 id="t-observation">What the Conjecture Is Trying to Explain</h2>
    <p>Webb has found an abundant early population of extremely compact sources with blue rest-ultraviolet
      light, steep red rest-optical continua and, in many examples, spectral evidence for accreting massive
      black holes. They become common in the first billion years and decline rapidly later. Dense gas around
      young black holes is presently the leading physical account of their unusual spectra.
      <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p>TTI does not replace that radiative-transfer account. It places a speculative geometric process beneath
      it: the cocoon is the material expression of an integration boundary, and the rapidly growing black hole
      is the centre around which the boundary closes. The observed redness still comes from atoms, gas,
      scattering, absorption and cosmological redshift. There is no special &ldquo;red frequency&rdquo; in the
      \(\chi\)-field or the negative interval.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">The interval the framework has not yet inhabited</p>
    <h2 id="t-negative-interval">What Could Exist Between Minus One and Zero?</h2>
    <p>The existing Cosmic Ledger uses \(R_{\infty}=-1\) as a <em>residue at infinity</em>: the balancing
      term required when the three positive residues sum to one. In mathematics, that minus sign is
      bookkeeping, not a hidden country. The interval \((-1,0)\) is simply another real interval, with the
      same cardinality as \((0,1)\). Negative numbers are not automatically earlier times or previous worlds.
      <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p>The new conjecture deliberately takes one step further. It treats \(-1\) not only as the closing
      residue but as the orientation-reversed image of completed \(+1\), and treats the open interval between
      \(-1\) and zero as a <strong>residue corridor</strong>: what remains after one universe has closed but
      before its surviving pattern has been given positive measure in another.</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \mathcal U_{n-1}
      \xrightarrow{\;\text{closure}\;}
      R_{\infty}^{(n-1)}=-1
      \longrightarrow
      \mathcal N=(-1,0)
      \xrightarrow{\;0^-\sim0^+\;}
      \mathcal U_n=(0,1).
    \]</p>
    <p>Zero is not crossed as an ordinary location. The two approaches \(0^-\) and \(0^+\) are identified
      as opposite faces of one seam, in the same spirit as the framework&rsquo;s Möbius return. The negative
      side carries orientation and residue; the positive side supplies measurable matter and a new causal
      history. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap">
  <div class="grid two">
    <div class="frame card illuminated">
      <p class="kicker">Reading A &mdash; literal ancestry</p>
      <h3>A remnant of a previous universe</h3>
      <p>A prior universe reaches closure, loses almost all local structure, and leaves a compressed residue
        at \(-1\). What crosses the zero seam is not an old galaxy with its stars intact but a seed condition:
        mass, topology, phase memory, information, or some combination still to be specified. A little red dot
        is the first luminous body assembled around one emergence point in the new universe.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <p class="kicker">Reading B &mdash; structural coexistence</p>
      <h3>A window into the \((-1,0)\) sector</h3>
      <p>No earlier universe is required. The negative interval can be a simultaneously existing,
        orientation-reversed sector of this universe. A little red dot then marks a local intersection where
        that hidden sector projects into positive measure. &ldquo;Previous&rdquo; describes its order in the
        closure map, not an earlier date on our cosmic clock.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">One residue, many red dots</p>
    <h3 id="t-one-to-many">The Multiplicity Need Not Create Many Prime Sources</h3>
    <p>If the previous universe has one globally transmissible Prime residue, hundreds of little red dots
      cannot each be a separate Prime Being arriving intact. The coherent version is one-to-many: one global
      negative-side boundary condition is sampled at many local emergence points, just as one field can seed
      many condensations.</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      R_{-}^{(n-1)}
      \xrightarrow{\;\mathcal K_{-+}\;}
      \bigl\{s_1^{(n)},s_2^{(n)},\ldots,s_N^{(n)}\bigr\},
      \qquad
      \text{one source residue, many local seeds}.
    \]</p>
    <p>The dots would share an ancestry without sharing an identity. They are not fragments of a person and
      do not multiply the Prime role. They are local physical consequences of one inherited global condition.
      This keeps the one-Prime-per-universe rule intact while allowing the sky to contain many punctures.
      <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
  </div>
</section>

