<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">Why early, why compact, why temporary?</p>
    <h2 id="t-timing">The New Universe Was Receiving and Building</h2>
    <p>TTI says dark matter is not inert material waiting beside ordinary matter. It is partial integration:
      the gravitational scaffold that makes complete integration possible. The inheritance conjecture adds
      that this scaffold may not be born wholly blank. In the first billion years, its steepest gradients may
      mark where negative-side boundary data are being resolved into positive structure.</p>
    <p>The full conjectured order is:</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \underbrace{r_-(\xi)}_{-1&lt;\xi&lt;0}
      \xrightarrow{\;\mathcal K_{-+}\;}
      \underbrace{\chi\simeq\tfrac12}_{\text{dark scaffold}}
      \longrightarrow
      \underbrace{\Sigma_{\rm LRD}}_{\text{compact seam}}
      \longrightarrow
      \underbrace{\chi\simeq1}_{\text{luminous host}}.
    \]</p>
    <p><strong>Early:</strong> inherited seeds would emerge most strongly near the new universe&rsquo;s initial
      seam. <strong>Compact:</strong> the residue would resolve first at the deepest closure centre, around the
      young black hole, before integration fills the host. <strong>Temporary:</strong> once the inherited
      condition is spent and the outer region reaches the completed phase, luminosity is distributed through
      a galaxy rather than trapped in one red point.</p>
    <p>This gives a sharper TTI reading of an apparently overmassive black hole in a faint host. The centre is
      not merely completing before its surroundings; it may begin with an inherited closure advantage. That
      possibility must predict more than rapid seed formation already can, or it adds only a story.
      <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">What would turn the picture into an explanation?</p>
    <h2 id="t-predictions">Six Debts the Sky Can Collect</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="grid two">
    <div class="frame card illuminated">
      <h3>One crossing law</h3>
      <p>Derive both \(\mathcal K_{-+}\) and \(D\chi/Dt\) from one action rather than choosing them object by
        object. The same law must fix where residues emerge and how long their luminous phase lasts.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>An inheritance clock</h3>
      <p>The supply from the zero seam must decay at a calculable rate. It should predict the observed early
        abundance and later decline, together with a source-to-host sequence as each luminous galaxy catches
        up with its compact inherited seed.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>A shared-residue signature</h3>
      <p>One global residue sampled many times should impose a population-level covariance: a constrained
        relation among seed mass, host deficit, duty cycle and environment that independent seed models do
        not produce. Similar redness alone is not enough.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card illuminated">
      <h3>A closed exchange ledger</h3>
      <p>The model must choose whether energy crosses. If it does, \(J_{-+}^{\nu}\) must balance on both
        branches without negative-mass shortcuts or double counting accretion. If it does not, all luminosity
        must be funded locally.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>An orientation trace</h3>
      <p>If the passage from \(-1\) to positive measure reverses orientation, the map should leave a fixed
        parity, handedness or phase relation in a statistical sample. The effect must be stated before looking
        at the data and separated from ordinary rotational selection.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>A way to lose</h3>
      <p>If ordinary seed formation explains every residual; if no stable crossing map exists; if the predicted
        handedness, redshift window or population covariance is absent; or if the energy budget fails, the
        inherited-residue explanation should be abandoned.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

