<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame frame--creed">
    <p class="kicker">The TTI reading, without costume</p>
    <h2 id="t-meaning">The Dot May Be a Memory Learning to Shine Again</h2>
    <p>The metaphysical image is now stronger. A universe closes but does not become nothing. Its final residue
      loses positive extension, traverses the interval before zero, and returns not as the same collection of
      things but as a bias toward becoming. At a few early knots, that memory gathers a new scaffold and the
      boundary itself shines. The little red dot is both a visible verb&mdash;<em>integrating</em>&mdash;and a
      possible echo: <em>remembering without remaining identical</em>.</p>
    <p><span class="myth">&#9670;</span> Those verbs belong to the philosophy of the framework. The proposal does
      not establish personal survival, communication from another world, or observational access to a spirit
      realm. Astronomy measures spectra, structure and dynamics. It could test only the physical shell around
      the image: a crossing law, conservation rule, orientation trace, population covariance and inheritance
      clock.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">Current astronomy in one paragraph</p>
    <h2 id="t-current">The Conventional Account Remains the Benchmark</h2>
    <p>The best-observed little red dots are increasingly consistent with accreting black holes enclosed by
      dense gas that absorbs, scatters and reprocesses their light; some have directly measured central masses,
      and faint stellar hosts are beginning to emerge in deeper or stacked imaging. The population is probably
      not perfectly uniform. None of this requires a \(\chi\)-transition, a previous universe or a
      \((-1,0)\) sector. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p>That is enough current theory for this page. Its purpose is not to reproduce the full astronomical
      debate but to expose what TTI would add, which parts merely rename known physics, and where the addition
      could be tested.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="https://science.nasa.gov/missions/webb/newfound-galaxy-class-may-indicate-early-black-hole-growth-webb-finds/">NASA Webb: the early little-red-dot population and black-hole evidence</a></li>
      <li><a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-09900-4">Rusakov et al.: young black holes in dense ionised cocoons</a></li>
      <li><a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-026-10579-4">Juodžbalis et al.: a direct black-hole mass in one little red dot</a></li>
      <li><a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/s41550-026-02945-z">Zhang et al.: extended stellar hosts around stacked little red dots</a></li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame frame--creed">
    <p class="lede">TTI&rsquo;s proposed answer is therefore not &ldquo;the black holes are illusions&rdquo; and not
      &ldquo;standard cosmology is broken&rdquo;. It is that a young black hole and its dense red cocoon may be the
      positive-side machinery through which an older residue becomes locally measurable: the interval from
      \(-1\) to zero supplies the inherited condition, while the \(\chi\)-front supplies its luminous body.
      The previous-universe account therefore extends the integration-front account; it does not replace the
      observed gas, accretion or host galaxy.</p>
